course: dictionary
lesson: 10354
title: Transport: moving people and goods
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Each new kind of transport solved a problem of the one before.» Here transport is a noun: a way of moving people or goods, such as cars, buses, trains, planes and ships.
explain: Transport can also be a verb, which is an action word. For example, a lorry can transport goods to the shops, so it moves them from one place to another. Ask yourself: is the word naming a thing, or telling you what someone does? That question helps you pick the right meaning.
explain: Read the words around it. «Boats are among the oldest kinds of transport.» Kinds tells you transport is a noun here. In science, «roots and stems transport water in a plant» means that a plant moves water from one part to another.
fact: Transport is a noun and a verb: the noun is a way of moving things, and the verb means to move them.
fact: «Transport» means moving people or goods from one place to another.
fact: «Transported» can also mean taken away in your mind by a story or music.
fact: In American English people often say «transportation» instead of transport.
fact: Plants and bodies have their own transport: blood carries oxygen around the body, and roots and stems carry water in a plant.
exercise: In «The new bridge will transport goods across the river», what kind of word is transport? | A noun | A place name | An adjective | *A verb
exercise: «Boats are among the oldest kinds of transport.» What does transport mean here? | To carry a heavy load up a hill | A kind of song | *A way of moving people or goods | A place where ships are built
exercise: «Roots and stems transport water in a plant.» What does transport mean in this science sentence? | To sell water in bottles | To stop water from flowing | *To move water from one place to another | To drink water quickly
test: «The heart and blood transport oxygen around the body.» What does transport mean here? | A kind of bus | *To carry or move something from place to place | To make oxygen | To breathe in
test: «Most old cities grew beside rivers, lakes or seas, which gave cheap transport for goods.» Why were water places useful to cities? | Rivers were used as roads for cars | *Boats were a cheap way to move goods | Rivers were always too deep to cross | Water made the air cleaner
exam: «A seed is a baby plant packed with a lunchbox… so plants have invented every kind of transport.» In this sentence, what does transport mean? | *Ways of moving seeds to new places | Ways of keeping cool | Ways of growing taller | Ways of drawing pictures
